// Formative practice quiz for login-with-a-session-cookie-or-jwt. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design. First draft, not yet reviewed.
$CATEGORY: react/auth-and-review/login-with-a-session-cookie-or-jwt

::login-with-a-session-cookie-or-jwt o1 - httponly::What does the HttpOnly attribute do for a session cookie?{
=Stops page scripts from reading it#Correct. That protects the cookie itself from theft by XSS.
~Encrypts the cookie contents#Not quite. It controls script access. It does not encrypt.
~Sends it only over HTTPS#Not quite. That is the Secure attribute.
~Stops the browser from sending it#Not quite. The browser still sends it with requests.
}

::login-with-a-session-cookie-or-jwt o1 - cookie risk::Since the browser attaches a session cookie automatically, which attack is the main concern?{
=Cross-site request forgery#Correct. Another site can trigger a request that carries the cookie. SameSite and CSRF tokens reduce this.
~Reading the cookie through document.cookie, when it is HttpOnly#Not quite. HttpOnly hides it from scripts.
~A slow network#Not quite. That is not a security attack.
~SQL injection in the browser#Not quite. That is a server-side database concern, and unrelated to how cookies are sent.
}

::login-with-a-session-cookie-or-jwt o1 - jwt readable::What is true of the contents of a typical signed JWT?{
=Anyone holding it can decode and read the claims#Correct. A signature protects integrity, not secrecy.
~They are encrypted by default#Not quite. A signed JWT is encoded, not encrypted.
~Only the browser can read them#Not quite. Any holder can decode them.
~They are a safe place for passwords#Not quite. They are readable, so never put secrets in them.
}

::login-with-a-session-cookie-or-jwt o2 - after login::A login succeeds. What should the front end do with the user data from the response?{
=Keep it in application state, such as a context, so screens can use it#Correct. Shared state lets any screen know who is signed in.
~Keep the password alongside it#Not quite. The password should not be kept.
~Nothing, because the server remembers#Not quite. The screen still needs to know who is signed in.
~Reload the page to apply it#Not quite. A reload would lose the state you have just received.
}

::login-with-a-session-cookie-or-jwt o3 - sending the credential::The back end uses a JWT. How does a later fetch carry it?{
=In an Authorization header, as Bearer followed by the token#Correct. That is the standard form for a bearer token.
~By setting credentials to include#Not quite. That is for cookies.
~In the URL query string#Not quite. URLs get logged and shared, so tokens should not go there.
~The browser adds it automatically#Not quite. Your code has to add a token held in JavaScript.
}
